 ```
 
-Exits 0 if every prompt is within tolerance (`--token-tol`, `--nll-tol`), 1
-otherwise. `--out` writes the per-prompt result as JSON.
+Exits 0 if the check passes, 1 otherwise. `--out` writes the per-prompt result
+as JSON.
+
+Bitwise equality is the right check only where the engine reproduces bitwise.
+A different TP degree, GPU, kernel choice or batch composition moves logprobs
+on a correct load (Llama-3.1-70B: up to 0.155 between the TP4 and TP8
+fixtures here), and an FP4 MoE may not reproduce at all. So the reference can
+carry its own noise floor:
+
+```bash
+# record, then measure the trusted server's noise: 1 repeat + 8 captures under perturbed load
+servekit verify --url http://127.0.0.1:8080 --record gold.json --calibrate 8 --weights
+
+# check: exact where the repeat reproduced bitwise, the calibrated test otherwise
+servekit verify --url http://127.0.0.1:8080 --reference gold.json
+```
+
+`--mode` picks the check:
+* `exact`: every logprob and greedy token bitwise equal. On a mismatch, it says whether the drift is inside the calibrated noise.
+* `statistical`: per-token drift normalized by the noise expected at that token's probability. It fails on three things: a token moved further than calibration allows (`max_z`); diffuse drift (`rms_z`, `shift`) beyond a prediction bound at false-alarm rate `--alpha` (default 0.01); and a top-1 or greedy token that changed where the reference's winner led by more than noise can close.
+* `auto` (default): `exact` if calibration showed the reference schedule reproduces bitwise, else `statistical`. An uncalibrated reference keeps the original fixed tolerances (`legacy`, `--token-tol`, `--nll-tol`).
+
+`--calibration-from` adds benign captures recorded elsewhere, such as another
+node, a restart or another TP degree, when the check must accept those
+differences too. `--weights` also records and compares SGLang's
+`/weights_checker` checksums of the live weights (SGLang >= 0.5.20). They are
+exact and noise-free, but they only cover tensors the module tree exposes.
+
+`python -m servekit.faults` writes corrupted copies of a checkpoint to prove a
+check catches them. It covers zeroed, duplicated, permuted or transposed TP
+shards, bit flips, missing tensors, FP8 block scales, MXFP4 E8M0 scales and
+swapped MoE experts, each at a chosen magnitude.
 
